From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 19:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] renderer: compose Dolby Vision as the composer of ETSI GS CCM
 001 does

libplacebo reconstructs a Dolby Vision picture per pixel at 4:4:4: the
chroma of the base layer is scaled up first, and the MMR mapping of the
chroma is evaluated at every pixel with the luma of that pixel; a code is
read as s / (2^n - 1); the enhancement layer is scaled up as a continuous
signal and its inverse quantization applied to the scaled values; the
output is clamped to the pivots. The composer of ETSI GS CCM 001 V1.1.1,
clause 5.4, is defined otherwise, in integers:
- 5.4.2.3.3: "the BL luma (colour component 0) shall be down-sampled, to
  align with chroma samples, before applying the MMR mapping process":
  the chroma is mapped per chroma sample (4:2:0), with
  s*_{i,j} = (((s_{2i-1,2j} + 2 s_{2i,2j} + s_{2i+1,2j} + 2) >> 2)
  + ((s_{2i-1,2j+1} + 2 s_{2i,2j+1} + s_{2i+1,2j+1} + 2) >> 2) + 1) >> 1;
- 5.4.2.3.2/3: a code s is read as s << (20 - BL_bit_depth) of 2^20, i.e.
  s / 2^n, the mapped signal v has 16 bits (v / 2^16), and the inputs,
  not the output, are clamped to the pivots;
- 5.4.3.2: the inverse quantization works on the integer codes of the
  enhancement layer, after a resampling that clause 4 (note 1) leaves out
  of the normative part and annex B (informative) gives a reference for;
- 5.4.3.3: h = (v + r + 2^(15 - hdr)) >> (16 - hdr), clamped to hdr bits.
On a profile 7 FEL stream the picture differs from libplacebo's by 4 to 15
codes of 4095 in almost every pixel (the normalization), and by up to 742
at edges of high luma contrast (the chroma composed at 4:4:4).

With the new pl_render_params.dovi_ccm the renderer composes a Dolby Vision
image as the clause does, in passes of its own before it reads the planes:
- the polynomial mapping of every code of the base layer, and the inverse
  quantization (NLQ_LINEAR_DZ) of every code the resampled layer can have,
  are tables made on the CPU with the clause's arithmetic (dovi_ccm.c);
- the MMR mapping is evaluated in the shader, exactly: with C' the
  coefficients scaled to 27 fractional bits, v = floor(sum C' tt / 2^27);
  the shader has floor(sum floor(C'/2^7) tt + floor(sum (C' mod 2^7) tt /
  2^7)) modulo 2^32 in 32-bit integers (the second sum exact in floats),
  hence v modulo 4096, and a float estimate within 2048 of v picks it;
  the products of the terms tt are shifted down exactly in 32 bits;
- an enhancement layer half the size of the base layer is resampled with
  the integer filters of annex B.3 (vertical, then horizontal, edges by
  replication), the geometry the composer of the clause is built around;
- the composed luma (per luma sample) and chroma (per chroma sample) hold
  h / 2^hdr_bit_depth and take the place of the image's planes: the chroma
  is scaled up by plane_upscaler at the chroma location of the base layer,
  and the decoding subtracts the ycc_to_rgb offsets as the RPU gives them
  (the composed signal is in their units, without the 2^n / (2^n - 1)).
The coefficients are needed as integers: pl_map_dovi_metadata() now also
fills pl_dovi_metadata.ccm with the composing metadata as the RPU codes it.

Two readings of the text, written where they are used:
- 5.4.2.2 starts pivot_idx at num_pivots_minus2 + 1, past the last piece,
  for a sample at or above the last pivot (code 1023 when that pivot is
  1023), which would then have no mapping: it takes the last piece; its
  value is clamped to the last pivot by the mapping either way;
- in s_{i,j} of a "(PicWidth)x(PicHeight)" block, "MxN (M-column by
  N-row)" (clause 3.1), i is the column: the [1 2 1] filter is horizontal,
  centred on the even columns, and two rows are averaged. The down-sampled
  luma is then co-sited with the chroma horizontally and centred between
  its two rows vertically: chroma sample location type 0 of H.273
  (chroma_sample_loc_type 0 of HEVC, inferred when a stream does not say),
  the default siting of 4:2:0, and the geometry of annex B too. The other
  reading would site it centred horizontally and co-sited vertically,
  type 3, which streams hardly use. (It changes 2562 Cb and 3662 Cr
  samples of one picture, by up to 307 codes of 4095. The filter of the
  clause does not depend on the siting a stream signals: the one profile 7
  stream at hand signals top-left, type 2.)

The MMR evaluation is exact while S = sum |C'| 2^20 / 2^27 over a piece
is below 2^28: the float estimate is then off by less than about 785
(49 * 2^-24 * S, with every float operation off by up to one ulp), well
inside the 2048 the choice allows. S is computed for every RPU on the CPU.

A picture the clause does not cover (MMR on the luma, a base layer that is
not 4:2:0, coefficient_log2_denom above 23, a layer of another size,
floating-point coefficients, film grain, interlacing, an MMR piece with
S >= 2^28) is composed as before, and each reason is logged once a stream
(until pl_renderer_flush_cache()), however the pictures alternate. A pass
that fails on the GPU sets the new PL_RENDER_ERR_DOVI_COMPOSER, the
picture is composed as before, and so are the following ones until the
error is reset. With pl_render_params.active_rows the passes leave out
the rows nothing reads; they still write every texel of their targets
(0 in the rows left out). Without dovi_ccm nothing changes.

Measured on Mesa's software OpenGL ES (zink on lavapipe), on top of
libplacebo-9913 and 9914 (exact weights, exact 1:1 read) and 9920 of the
tree, against two transcriptions of the clause written apart (C and numpy,
64-bit integers, annex B.3 for the layer), which give the same samples on
every picture:
- the composed planes are identical to them, every sample, on 8 pictures
  of a profile 7 FEL stream and on 12 made-up cases (MMR of order 1, 2
  and 3, a component with MMR and polynomial pieces, polynomials of order
  2 with the pivots inside the code range, 8-bit layers,
  coefficient_log2_denom 18, hdr_bit_depth 10, a layer of the base layer's
  size, NLQ with a threshold and with hdr_in_max clamping, no residual,
  a random base layer), each with planar and with P010 textures; a 13th
  case, beyond the exact range, is composed as before;
- the bound, both ways, with made-up MMR pieces of order 3 whose terms
  cancel (95 % of the chroma samples in range): with S = 2^28 - 2^-7 the
  composed planes are identical to the transcriptions; with S = 2^28 the
  picture is byte for byte the one without dovi_ccm, and alternating the
  two logs each once, and again after pl_renderer_flush_cache(). On the
  2878 pictures of the stream S is at most 2^23.6, 21 times below the
  bound; the float estimate, emulated in float32 in the shader's order on
  300 random sets at the bound, is off by at most 49;
- the reconstructed picture differs from the reference's (the stage after
  the composer in 64-bit floats) by one code of 4095 in 0.01 to 0.68 % of
  the pixels of the stream's pictures, never by more (libplacebo's
  reshaping: almost every pixel of the active picture, by up to 742);
- Kodi's Dolby Vision renderer passes its render test, and its two ways
  (composed, and drawn and packed in one shader) give the same transport
  bytes in all 48 drawings of the 8 pictures; with and without
  active_rows (the test's early hook) the transport and the composed
  picture are the same in every drawing of 106 runs (5088 drawings), and
  the composed planes, in the rows composed, are those of a full
  composition, even after another picture was drawn with the same
  renderer, with 0 in the rows left out. One earlier run, of a build of
  this patch that summed the low parts of the MMR coefficients in 32-bit
  integers (since replaced by the exact float sums), drew transport bytes
  that differed with and without the early hook (1 to 91 bytes in 20 of
  48 drawings); in 160 runs since, on this base and on one without 9913
  and 9914, with the dumps of the test on and off, 10 of them with that
  build rebuilt, not one byte differed.

License: LGPL-2.1-or-later, that of libplacebo and of the files changed,
whose notices stay as they are; dovi_ccm.c and dovi_ccm.h carry it too.
